A||||7||kempo||Fiero runs cool, thermostat problems||||1||
Z||000000||kempo||11-29-2001||01:27 PM||||My 87GT V6 is having some issues.  The temp need hardly moves (not stuck) and I don't think that the engine is warming up properly.  When I switch on the heat for the cold mornings, it takes forever for it to actually warm up, on a higher setting of 3 or 4, and actually starts getting warm when I am about 3/5ths the way to where I go in the mornings.  <P>The cap was on there so tight, I could just barely get it off.  After I did, I looked at the thermostat- looked as it should, all together and all.  I don't think it was fit in there properly, as that would indicate why I had such a hard time getting the cap off.  I purchased a new thermostat, and am running into a similar problem with it.  I don't know how far down it should be going, but the cap barely wants to go on, and if forced on, won't go all the way.  I tried to feel if it could have something preventing the thermostat from sliding down further, to which little avail.  Should the thermostat slide down enough to allow the cap to be easily placed and tightened, or is it normal to really have to force the thing on there?  I figured that the heat would be related to the car not warming up to where it ought to be.  Is my judgement accurate, or should I assume that I may need to get a new heater core?  No antifreeze/coolant leeks into the interior of the car, as I've seen happen on others.  Please help me out!<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.admist.net/gtsmall.jpg">    <BR>0-60 6.9 seconds||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||11-29-2001||01:34 PM||||Sometimes thermostat caps can be on there pretty good, usually if it hasent been touched in a long time and the heat of the coolant has cause the rubber seal on the bottom to fuse itself to the thermostat housing.<P>After the cap has been wrestled off the first time, they are usually easy to take on and off as long as the rubber seal on the bottom of the cap is not binding up.<P>What sounds like is happening is that you don't have the thermostat seated all the way down, and coolant is allowed to flow at all times, causeing and extended time for the engine to warm up.  If you have really really forced the cap on there and the termostat was the cause of the interference, you may have bent or damaged your new thermostat.<P>When installing a new thermostat, gently push it down into the housing, and then use a flat blade screw driver to press it down all the way.  Press down around the base of the thermostat, and press all the way around it to make sure it is seated flat.  It should kinda snap into there.<P>Go check your thermostat and see if it is seated all the way, and check to make sure that your cap seal is not binding either.||||reg||1
Z||000002||kempo||11-29-2001||04:00 PM||||FieroJoe- thanks for the quickie reply.  I have taken your advice and cleaned the thing out as best I could as well.  The thermostat was carefully taken care of and is not damaged in any way.  I got the cap to close to the fullest, but it was a royal pain to do.  <P>On to my next question, probably should start a new thread on this, but how can I tell if my Temp guage is royally f'd?  It only moves a hair from the cold mark and stays there, even when the engine is really warm.  On a hot day, say 85-90º outside, it would move a little more than that, but not much.  The fan rarely turns on.  I was hoping the thermostat would help with this.  I was wrong.  I am going to look into an aftermarket temp guage and wire up a fan toggle switch.  Before I do that, is there any way to fix the temp guage, IF ANYTHING is wrong with it that we can figure out?  Out of the six fiero's I've owned (mix combo of Iron Duke and V6), only ONE had a temp guage that I believed to work properly.  How common of a problem is this?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Phil||11-29-2001||04:30 PM||||Does the gauge peg when you first start the car?||||reg||1
Z||000004||kempo||11-29-2001||04:37 PM||||Phil, what exactly do you mean?  The guage is pegged when the fiero is off and not running.  When the ignition is on, it goes to where the needle thinks that the engine should be.  When I go to start it, the needle jumps around while cranking (normal), and then goes to cold, or where it thinks that the engine temp is.  I disagree with it's reading as I think that the fiero is actually hotter than the guage readout.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fldevel||11-29-2001||06:42 PM||||ive had the same problem before it was the lil rubber gasket on the thermo i just baught a new one and installed it problem solved ..||||reg||1
